window.EXPLAINER_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"k":"page","t":"The central files: a map of the files that carry the logic","u":"code/central-files.html","s":"Nineteen files carry most of dsh-superagent's own logic. This page gives each one's size, its role, and its main export, grouped by folder.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"","h":["Purpose","The domain core (src/core/)","Policy (src/)","Execution (src/executor/)","The app layer (src/app/)","Source plugins (src/sources/)","The panel's data (src/client/)","Where to read more","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Code map: the folder tree","u":"code/index.html","s":"Every folder of the repository at the pinned commit, with file and line counts, a short description, and links to the module and concept pages.","sec":"Code map","lvl":"","h":["Purpose","The folder tree","How the numbers were measured","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Effects and the Store: a durable queue, and one writer per state","u":"concepts/effects-and-store.html","s":"Effects is the durable queue behind launches, deliveries and replies. Store is the one SQLite file behind it, where each kind of state has exactly one writer.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: enqueue writes a row; a handler reads it back after commit","Step 2: each of the six kinds has exactly one handler","Step 3: Engine and Outbox are each the sole writer of their own state","More detail","What a restart recovers, and what it does not","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The Executor and worker Sessions: one Session per Ticket, a concurrency limit","u":"concepts/executor-and-worker-sessions.html","s":"DshExecutor gives every accepted Ticket one worker Session and one directory. WorkerSlots caps how many may run at once, with one slot kept for a person's message.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: the Executor port only queues the work","Step 2: WorkerSlots reserves a slot before anything starts","Step 3: a Ticket keeps its slot through background work","More detail","What ends a worker's turn, and what does not","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The panel and the Management API: tabs, routes and settings versions","u":"concepts/panel-and-management-api.html","s":"A React panel injected into DSH's web sidebar calls a loopback-only Management API; each settings block and prompt revision saves with its own version number.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: one HTTP prefix, checked for loopback and same origin","Step 2: six tabs, one concept per tab","Step 3: a settings save versions against a block, not the whole object","Step 4: some fields only the panel itself may change","More detail","Why a prompt upgrade needs a human choice, not a silent merge","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Policy and authorization: the gate, Eval and the Mandate it grants","u":"concepts/policy-and-authorization.html","s":"Before a Ticket exists, the gate checks who may send a Trigger, Eval judges the batch, and only an accept decision grants a Mandate.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: the gate judges each queued Trigger","Step 2: Eval records a Decision for the gate's result","Step 3: an accept decision builds the Mandate","Step 4: strength, and what the admission table decides","More detail","What each Mandate basis means before a resume","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Prompts and Source skills: how the system prompt is assembled","u":"concepts/prompts-and-skills.html","s":"dsh-superagent builds each role's system prompt again for each model request, from built-in parts, three team sections and generated parts, in a fixed order. Two bundled Source skills join each Session's skill scope.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: what exists to assemble","Step 2: the fixed join order","Step 3: which text a built-in part actually uses","Step 4: the two bundled Source skills, and team skills","More detail","How the main Session edits and previews prompts","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Source, Trigger, Thread and Channel: where work comes from","u":"concepts/sources-and-triggers.html","s":"A Source turns one native event into one immutable Trigger; Triggers in time order form a Thread, and a Channel writes a reply back.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: a Source kind declares its shape; a Source instance configures one","Step 2: a native event becomes a Trigger","Step 3: Triggers join a Thread, and a Subscription fans them out","Step 4: a Channel is the Thread's write side","More detail","What reaching a Ticket does, and does not, grant","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"The Ticket lifecycle: stages, Assignments and Subscriptions","u":"concepts/ticket-lifecycle.html","s":"A Ticket moves through a fixed set of stages as its Assignments and Subscriptions change. This page follows one Ticket from accept to end, with the stage machine's own file and lines.","sec":"dsh-superagent concepts","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","In the code","Step 1: an accept creates the Ticket, its Mandate and its first Assignment","Step 2: the stage machine reads what the Ticket already has","Step 3: an Assignment moves from pending to running to ended","Step 4: a Subscription delivers later Triggers as Inputs, with no new Eval","More detail","Why retry and resume never create a new Ticket","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"dsh-superagent Explained","u":"index.html","s":"A study guide to this codebase for developers who are new to it. It starts simple and goes down to the exact code.","sec":"Home","lvl":"","h":["The system map","Start here","How to use this site","The code version this site explains"]},{"k":"page","t":"What dsh-superagent is","u":"learn/what-this-is.html","s":"dsh-superagent runs on DSH and Cordis, and it adds its own domain model on top: Source, Trigger, Policy, Eval, Ticket, Executor and the panel.","sec":"Learn the basics","lvl":"Beginner","h":["The idea","The picture","In dsh-superagent","What the sibling site already teaches","What dsh-superagent adds","More detail","Why GitHub and Feishu share one Source contract","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Configuration and deployment: the bundle, settings, env vars, the CLI and the API","u":"reference/config-and-deployment.html","s":"How dsh-superagent loads as a DSH bundle, each settings block's defaults, the execution environment variables, the scripts/dsh-superagent CLI, and the Management API's routes.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"","h":["Purpose","How the bundle loads into a DSH profile","Settings blocks and their defaults","Execution environment variables","The scripts/dsh-superagent CLI","The Management API","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Life of a GitHub mention: from a comment to a merged pull request","u":"tours/life-of-a-github-mention.html","s":"One GitHub comment that mentions the agent, followed stop by stop through Intake, the gate, Eval, a Ticket, a pull request and the Ticket's end.","sec":"Guided tours","lvl":"Intermediate","h":["The idea","The picture","1A GitHub comment mentions the agent","2Intake ingests the Trigger","3The gate admits it as explicit","4Eval batches the Thread and asks the assessor","5The main Session routes an accept","6Eval's accept builds the Mandate and asks for a Ticket","7Tickets.create() builds the Ticket and starts the Executor","8The acknowledgement goes back to GitHub","9The Executor launches the worker Session","What if every worker slot is already busy?","10The worker Session works, and opens a pull request","11The worker reports ready, naming its pull request","12The report is recorded; the Ticket waits for review","13GitHub observes the pull request close, and the Ticket ends","What can go wrong","Check yourself","Related pages"]},{"k":"page","t":"Glossary","u":"reference/glossary.html","s":"Short definitions of every technical term on this site, with links to the pages that explain them.","sec":"Reference","lvl":"","h":["A","B","C","D","E","M","O","P","R","S","T","W"]},{"k":"term","t":"accept mode","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-accept-mode","s":"The Policy setting that controls inferred candidates: off, observe (assess but never accept) or auto (assess and accept).","a":["accept-mode"]},{"k":"term","t":"admission","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-admission","s":"Eval's first, deterministic layer: it decides who may send a Trigger, with what strength, before any assessment runs.","a":["gate","the gate","admission table"]},{"k":"term","t":"Assignment","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-assignment","s":"The binding of a Ticket to one Executor instance. A Ticket has several over time. Its state is pending, running or ended.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"built-in part","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-built-in-part","s":"One named built-in text, such as runtime/admission or a Source skill, that a team may override with its own text.","a":["built-in parts","prompt part"]},{"k":"term","t":"Channel","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-channel","s":"The write side of a Thread: where a reply, acknowledgement or result goes back to the Source.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Decision","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-decision","s":"The result of an Eval: accept, stop, ignore or unavailable, with its reasons.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"DSH bundle","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-dsh-bundle","s":"How dsh-superagent ships: a package with a Cordis patch file that a DSH profile loads, not a standalone program.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"DSH profile","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-dsh-profile","s":"A named DeepSeek Harness configuration and runtime. dsh-superagent runs in its own profile, separate from a person's own.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Effect","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-effect","s":"One durable row in the side-effect queue: an assessment, a launch, a delivery, a relay, a notice or a reply, run after its transaction commits.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Eval","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-eval","s":"The judgement on a batch of Triggers: a gate layer, then an assessment, ending in a Decision.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Executor","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-executor","s":"The port that runs Assignments. Its implementation keeps one Executor Session and one directory per Ticket.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Management API","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-management-api","s":"The local HTTP API the panel uses, and that scripts and agents can also call, under one path prefix.","a":["orchestrator API"]},{"k":"term","t":"Mandate","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-mandate","s":"What an accept grants: the strength, scope and Policy version a Ticket's work relies on. It never changes once set.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Outbox","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-outbox","s":"The Engine's port for Channel writes: it keeps a receipt for every outgoing message and sends it as durable work.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Panel","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-panel","s":"The React page dsh-superagent injects into DeepSeek Harness's own web sidebar, for supervising and configuring it.","a":["management panel","web panel"]},{"k":"term","t":"Policy","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-policy","s":"The versioned rules for who may start work, and each Source's own part of those rules.","a":["Policy version"]},{"k":"term","t":"prompt revision","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-prompt-revision","s":"One saved, numbered version of a role's prompt configuration: the team sections, overrides and skills, together.","a":["prompt version"]},{"k":"term","t":"Report","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-report","s":"What an Assignment reports back to its Ticket: progress, waiting, ready, blocked, failed or dropped.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"settings block","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-settings-block","s":"One named part of Settings, such as policy, executor or one Source, saved on its own with its own revision counter.","a":["settings blocks"]},{"k":"term","t":"Source","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-source","s":"A stream of events. A Source kind is a reusable plugin; a Source instance is one configured kind, such as one GitHub repository.","a":["Source kind","Source instance"]},{"k":"term","t":"Source skill","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-source-skill","s":"A bundled Skill that teaches the agents a Source's own tools, such as GitHub's gh CLI or reading a Feishu chat.","a":["bundled skill","bundled Source skill"]},{"k":"term","t":"stage","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-stage","s":"A Ticket's current wait: idle, working (plain or queued), person (delivery, answer, review, blocked or failed), or ended.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Store","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-store","s":"The one SQLite database that holds every domain aggregate, the effect queue and the Outbox's receipts.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"strength","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-strength","s":"How a Trigger was admitted: explicit, inferred, or not admitted (no Mandate at all).","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Subscription","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-subscription","s":"The many-to-many link between Tickets and Threads. A Trigger on a subscribed Thread reaches every live subscribed Ticket.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"team section","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-team-section","s":"One of three prompt sections a team writes directly: Communication, Ticket scope and Delivery requirements.","a":["team sections"]},{"k":"term","t":"Thread","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-thread","s":"Triggers in time order. At most one Eval runs on a Thread at a time, and a Thread may carry several Tickets.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Ticket","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-ticket","s":"A unit of authorized work. Only an Eval's accept creates one; it goes open to ended and sometimes back to open.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"Trigger","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-trigger","s":"One immutable event a Source produces, such as a GitHub comment or a Feishu message, with the Source's own metadata.","a":[]},{"k":"term","t":"worker slot","u":"reference/glossary.html#term-worker-slot","s":"One unit of the Executor's concurrency limit. A Ticket holds one while its worker launches, runs a turn, or finishes background work.","a":[]}];
